```go
func mergeSorted(a, b []int) []int {
    out := make([]int, 0, len(a)+len(b))
    i, j := 0, 0
    for i < len(a) && j < len(b) {
        if a[i] <= b[j] {
            out = append(out, a[i])
            i++
        } else {
            out = append(out, b[j])
            j++
        }
    }
    out = append(out, a[i:]...)
    out = append(out, b[j:]...)
    return out
}
```

Two pointers walk both slices, always taking the smaller head. Using `<=` keeps the merge stable for equal values. Preallocating with `make` at the exact final capacity avoids repeated growth copies, and once one input is exhausted the other's remainder is appended in one call. Runs in O(len(a)+len(b)) time.

```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func worker(id int, jobs <-chan int, results chan<- int, wg *sync.WaitGroup) {
    defer wg.Done()
    for j := range jobs {
        results <- j * j
    }
}

func main() {
    jobs := make(chan int, 5)
    results := make(chan int, 5)

    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for w := 1; w <= 3; w++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go worker(w, jobs, results, &wg)
    }

    for j := 1; j <= 5; j++ {
        jobs <- j
    }
    close(jobs)

    go func() {
        wg.Wait()
        close(results)
    }()

    for r := range results {
        fmt.Println(r)
    }
}
```

The pieces that make this idiomatic: workers signal completion through a `WaitGroup`, not sleeps; `close(jobs)` is how workers learn there is no more work (their `range` ends); and a separate goroutine closes `results` only after all workers finish, so the main loop can `range` over results without knowing the count in advance. Note the result order is not guaranteed — whichever worker finishes first sends first.

Show me how to write a proper table-driven test in Go for this function:

func clamp(v, lo, hi int) int {
    if v < lo { return lo }
    if v > hi { return hi }
    return v
}

Table-driven tests are the Go idiom: cases are data, the test body is one loop.

```go
func TestClamp(t *testing.T) {
    tests := []struct {
        name       string
        v, lo, hi  int
        want       int
    }{
        {"within range", 5, 0, 10, 5},
        {"below low", -3, 0, 10, 0},
        {"above high", 42, 0, 10, 10},
        {"at low edge", 0, 0, 10, 0},
        {"at high edge", 10, 0, 10, 10},
        {"negative range", -7, -10, -5, -7},
    }
    for _, tt := range tests {
        t.Run(tt.name, func(t *testing.T) {
            if got := clamp(tt.v, tt.lo, tt.hi); got != tt.want {
                t.Errorf("clamp(%d, %d, %d) = %d, want %d",
                    tt.v, tt.lo, tt.hi, got, tt.want)
            }
        })
    }
}
```

Three details matter: each case gets a `name` and runs as a subtest via `t.Run`, so failures identify the exact case (`go test -run TestClamp/at_low_edge` reruns just one); the error message includes the inputs, not just got/want, so a failure is self-explaining; and the edge cases (exactly at lo, exactly at hi) are in the table — boundary values are where clamp-style functions break.

