```c
#include <string.h>

void reverse(char *s) {
    size_t n = strlen(s);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n / 2; i++) {
        char tmp = s[i];
        s[i] = s[n - 1 - i];
        s[n - 1 - i] = tmp;
    }
}
```

Swap the outermost pair, move inward, stop at the middle — `n / 2` iterations handles both parities: for even lengths every element swaps once; for odd lengths the middle character is correctly left alone. It works in place with O(1) extra space because the caller hands us a mutable buffer.

One warning that matters in C: this must never be called on a string literal (`reverse("abc")`) — literals may live in read-only memory and writing to them is undefined behavior (usually a segfault). The parameter being `char *` rather than `const char *` is the conventional signal of that.

