Use-after-free is dereferencing a pointer whose memory has been returned to the allocator. The pointer still holds the old address — nothing about it looks wrong — but that memory may now belong to a different allocation.

```c
char *msg = malloc(64);
strcpy(msg, "session opened");
free(msg);

// ... later, elsewhere in the program:
log_write(msg);          // use after free
char *other = malloc(64); // may well receive the SAME block
strcpy(other, "user input");
printf("%s", msg);        // prints user input — or crashes, or works
```

Why it's worse than a crash: freeing doesn't erase or protect the memory, it just puts the block on a free list. If nothing reuses it yet, the buggy code *appears to work* — the old bytes are still there. When an unrelated `malloc` later reclaims the block, your stale pointer now aliases live data belonging to someone else: reads leak their data, writes corrupt it. That's why UAF bugs are security-critical, not just crashes.

Defenses: set pointers to `NULL` immediately after `free` (turns later misuse into a clean, debuggable segfault instead of silent aliasing); have exactly one owner responsible for freeing; and run tests under AddressSanitizer (`-fsanitize=address`), which quarantines freed blocks and reports the first invalid access with both the free site and the use site.

