This C loop sometimes prints garbage as the last element:

int a[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
for (int i = 0; i <= 5; i++) {
    printf("%d\n", a[i]);
}

The loop runs six times (i = 0…5) over a five-element array whose valid indices are 0…4. On the last iteration it reads `a[5]`, which is outside the array — undefined behavior. In practice it prints whatever happens to sit next to `a` in memory, which is why the garbage varies and why it sometimes looks fine.

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    printf("%d\n", a[i]);
}
```

Better, tie the bound to the array so it can't drift out of sync when the size changes:

```c
for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof a / sizeof a[0]; i++) { ... }
```

The `<=` vs `<` slip is the single most common C loop bug because both bounds *look* plausible: `5` is the length, and the length feels like it should be included. It isn't — length is a count, indices stop one earlier. And unlike Java or Python, C won't throw anything: no bounds check exists, so the failure mode is silent garbage or corruption rather than a clean exception.

Review this C allocation code for security problems:

uint32_t count = read_count_from_packet(packet);
Item *items = malloc(count * sizeof(Item));
for (uint32_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    items[i] = parse_item(packet, i);
}

The multiplication can overflow before `malloc` ever sees it. `count` comes from the network, so an attacker controls it. `count * sizeof(Item)` is computed in `uint32_t` (or `size_t` on 32-bit) arithmetic: pick `count` so the product wraps around — e.g. with `sizeof(Item)` = 16, a count of 0x10000001 makes the product wrap to a tiny value. `malloc` happily allocates the small wrapped size, then the loop writes `count` items into it: a massive heap overflow with attacker-controlled contents. This exact pattern is a recurring CVE class.

Fix by checking before multiplying:

```c
if (count > SIZE_MAX / sizeof(Item)) {
    return NULL; // would overflow
}
Item *items = malloc(count * sizeof(Item));
if (items == NULL) {
    return NULL; // also missing in the original: malloc can fail
}
```

Also worth fixing while you're here: the original never checks `malloc`'s return, so an ordinary out-of-memory becomes a null dereference in the loop. And validate `count` against a sane protocol maximum first — a packet claiming 4 billion items is wrong regardless of overflow. Defenses stack: semantic limit, overflow check, null check. (`calloc(count, sizeof(Item))` performs the overflow check internally on glibc and is a reasonable alternative, but the explicit check documents intent.)

